WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.200
Authentication and session management are central to Node.js application security because

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.780
many services handle users,

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:10.750
API clients, service accounts, administrators, and automated jobs.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:14.100
Password handling should rely on appropriate password hashing, policy,

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.740
and reset flows rather than custom storage ideas.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:21.000
Multi-factor authentication can reduce account takeover risk when designed and operated

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.825
with clear recovery processes.

00:00:22.825 --> 00:00:26.640
Session cookies and JSON Web Tokens solve different problems and create different risks.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.627
Cookies can be protected with attributes that reduce exposure in browser contexts,

00:00:30.627 --> 00:00:34.140
but they need CSRF consideration when automatically sent by the browser.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:38.980
JWTs can be useful for stateless or distributed designs, but teams must manage signing,

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:44.180
validation, audience, issuer, expiration, rotation, and revocation expectations carefully.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:48.108
Token lifetime matters. Long-lived access tokens increase exposure when leaked.

00:00:48.108 --> 00:00:51.580
Refresh tokens need stronger protection because they can extend access.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:55.340
API keys should be scoped, rotated, monitored, and stored securely.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:59.300
The server should validate every request based on current policy rather than trusting that

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.805
possession of a token automatically answers every authorization question.

00:01:02.805 --> 00:01:06.990
Secrets should not be hard-coded in source code or casually placed in logs, build output,

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:10.855
container images, client bundles, or CI/CD configuration.

00:01:10.855 --> 00:01:13.220
Environment variables can be part of a secret strategy,

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.860
but they are not a complete security program by themselves.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:19.460
Secret managers, restricted access, rotation, audit logging,

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:23.800
and safe deployment practices help keep secrets from becoming permanent hidden

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:24.160
liabilities.
